+ksmbd symbolic link tests
+========================
+
+Representation and path resolution
+----------------------------------
+
+ksmbd exposes existing filesystem symbolic links as SMB symbolic-link reparse
+points. Links created through ``FSCTL_SET_REPARSE_POINT`` instead store their
+target in the private ``user.ksmbd.symlink`` extended attribute of an empty
+regular file or empty directory. The attribute contains the local target bytes
+without a terminating NUL. Setting it preserves the backing inode and existing
+open handles; it does not replace the object with a filesystem symbolic link.
+Consequently, local filesystem tools still see the backing file or directory.
+
+SMB queries present either representation as a symbolic link. A normal open
+stops at the first link, including a link in an intermediate path component,
+and returns ``STATUS_STOPPED_ON_SYMLINK`` with the target and unparsed suffix.
+The client resolves the target. An open with ``FILE_OPEN_REPARSE_POINT`` can
+query the link itself using ``FSCTL_GET_REPARSE_POINT``. The server does not
+follow these targets to access the server's filesystem.
+
+Both POSIX and non-POSIX connections use ``IO_REPARSE_TAG_SYMLINK``. POSIX
+connections preserve Unix target syntax, including absolute targets; an
+absolute target refers to the client's root. Non-POSIX connections support
+relative targets and translate path separators to Windows syntax. Absolute
+targets are unsupported in non-POSIX mode. A literal backslash in a POSIX
+target cannot be used reliably across both modes, because non-POSIX clients
+interpret it as a separator.
+
+The private attribute requires backing filesystem support for user extended
+attributes. Backups and restores must preserve it together with the backing
+object. This format is not interchangeable with Samba's stored reparse-point
+format. Native filesystem symbolic links do not use this attribute. Magic
+links such as those in procfs are unsupported.
+
+Manual Linux client tests
+-------------------------
+
+Use a test server and client with the changed ksmbd and a Linux CIFS client
+supporting ``symlink=native``. Create a dedicated fixture on the server as the
+Unix user mapped to the test SMB account::
+
+    share_dir=$(mktemp -d /tmp/ksmbd-symlink.XXXXXX)
+    mkdir "$share_dir/subdir"
+    printf 'file target\n' > "$share_dir/file"
+    printf 'directory target\n' > "$share_dir/subdir/file"
+    ln -s file "$share_dir/native"
+    ln -s subdir "$share_dir/native-dir"
+    ln -s missing "$share_dir/dangling"
+    printf '%s\n' "$share_dir"
+
+Export that directory as a writable ``symlink-test`` share for an existing
+test account, here named ``smbtest``. Enable SMB3 POSIX extensions on the server
+for the POSIX pass. Run the following on the test client as root, replacing
+``SERVER`` with the server address. Mount prompts for the account password::
+
+    mount_dir=$(mktemp -d /tmp/ksmbd-symlink-mnt.XXXXXX)
+    for mode in noposix posix; do
+        mount -t cifs //SERVER/symlink-test "$mount_dir" \
+            -o "vers=3.1.1,username=smbtest,symlink=native,$mode" || break
+        ls -l "$mount_dir"
+        readlink "$mount_dir/native"
+        cat "$mount_dir/native"
+        cat "$mount_dir/native-dir/file"
+        readlink "$mount_dir/dangling"
+        ln -s file "$mount_dir/remote-$mode"
+        ln -s subdir "$mount_dir/remote-dir-$mode"
+        ln -s missing "$mount_dir/remote-dangling-$mode"
+        readlink "$mount_dir/remote-$mode"
+        readlink "$mount_dir/remote-dangling-$mode"
+        cat "$mount_dir/remote-$mode"
+        cat "$mount_dir/remote-dir-$mode/file"
+        ls -l "$mount_dir"
+        umount "$mount_dir" || break
+    done
+
+Check each command's result before continuing after a failure. ``readlink``
+must return the stored relative target, and ``cat`` must print the matching
+fixture content. Dangling links must remain visible to ``ls -l`` and
+``readlink``; opening their missing targets must fail. File modes and ownership
+reported without POSIX extensions need not match those on a POSIX mount.
+
+Remount each mode and repeat the queries to check persistence and access to
+links created in the other mode. On the server, inspect a remotely created
+link with ``getfattr -n user.ksmbd.symlink "$share_dir/remote-posix"`` and verify
+that its target is ``file``. Local ``readlink`` on that backing object should
+not report a native link. Keep these fixtures for inspection; unmount the
+client before removing the dedicated test directories.
+
+Additional protocol coverage
+----------------------------
+
+Exercise the following with an SMB request generator or integration harness:
+
+* ``GET_REPARSE_POINT`` on native and stored links, including dangling links;
+  normal opens through intermediate links with non-ASCII names and suffixes.
+* ``SET_REPARSE_POINT`` on an empty regular file and an empty directory;
+  reject nonempty objects, the share root, streams and malformed buffers
+  without deleting the backing object or its contents.
+* Link-handle reads, writes, truncation, allocation, zeroing and copying,
+  including the original handle used for ``SET_REPARSE_POINT``. These must
+  not operate on the backing data as ordinary file contents.
+* POSIX absolute targets resolving at the client, and rejection of absolute
+  targets through non-POSIX connections. Use a dedicated client fixture.
+* Renaming and removing links without changing their targets. A stored
+  directory containing locally added entries must not lose those entries.
+* Generic SMB extended-attribute requests must not expose or modify the
+  private ``user.ksmbd.symlink`` attribute.
+
+KUnit
+-----
+
+For a kernel satisfying the ksmbd and tmpfs prerequisites, enable::
+
+    CONFIG_KUNIT=y
+    CONFIG_SMB_SERVER=y
+    CONFIG_SMB_KUNIT_TESTS=y
+    CONFIG_SMB_SERVER_KUNIT_TESTS=y
+    CONFIG_TMPFS=y
+    CONFIG_TMPFS_XATTR=y
+
+Build and boot the test kernel, or use the KUnit runner described in
+``Documentation/dev-tools/kunit/``. Inspect the ``ksmbd-reparse`` suite results.
+The suite checks target parsing, malformed reparse buffers and length limits.
+It does not replace the mount, VFS persistence or protocol tests above. These
+instructions describe validation to perform; they do not record a completed
+end-to-end test run.
